Licence for spec/
=================

This directory holds two kinds of file under two licences. The boundary
is by file type, and it is the same boundary everywhere it appears in
this repository.

PROSE — Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International (CC BY 4.0)
------------------------------------------------------------------

    scf-spec.md
    conformance.md
    stability.md
    CHANGELOG.md
    ARTIFACTS.md
    (and any other .md in this directory)

    SPDX-License-Identifier: CC-BY-4.0
    Copyright 2023-2026 Christopher Smallfield and Jesse Kretschmer

Full licence text: https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode
Summary:           https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/

You may share and adapt these documents, including commercially,
provided you give appropriate credit, link to the licence, and indicate
whether changes were made. You may not suggest that the licensor
endorses you or your use.

A specification nobody may quote is a specification nobody adopts. CC BY
is what a format written to be implemented by other people should carry:
it lets an implementer paste a rule into their own documentation, lets a
standards body cite it, and lets someone else carry the format on if
this project stops (GOVERNANCE.md).

GENERATED DATA — Apache License 2.0
-----------------------------------

    scf-schema.sql
    screenplay-tables.json
    registry.schema.json
    finding-catalog.json
    junction-keys.json
    readiness-rubrics.json
    scf.magic
    SHA256SUMS

    SPDX-License-Identifier: Apache-2.0
    Copyright 2023-2026 Christopher Smallfield and Jesse Kretschmer

Full licence text: ../LICENSE

These are not prose. Every one is generated from code in schema/ or
scf-core/, regenerated on every change and verified by CI, and they are
consumed by programs rather than read by people. They carry the licence
of the code that produces them, because that is what they are: output.

The same reasoning puts fixtures/ under Apache-2.0 — the conformance
fixture, the sixteen query results and the eleven negative reports are
data an implementer needs to embed in a test suite, and a share-alike or
attribution burden on a test fixture is a burden on conformance itself.

WHY TWO LICENCES
----------------

Apache-2.0 is written for software. It speaks of source and object form,
of derivative works and patent grants, and its attribution requirements
are shaped around distributing a program. Applied to a specification it
is at best awkward and at worst unclear about the thing that matters
most: whether you may quote it.

CC BY 4.0 is written for documents and answers that question directly.

Neither licence is copyleft, so nothing here obliges anyone to open their
implementation.

WHAT IS NOT LICENSED HERE
-------------------------

corpus/ is screenplay material and is covered by corpus/README.md and
the repository's root LICENSE. Do not assume the licence of this
directory extends to it.
